# Chroma library, modern Earth: WAYS TO LEAD (S7, v22.3, switch lead_ways; cast_want lead_take / lead_crisis /
# lead_fall / lead_routine / lead_hand). The character leads at {place} one of five ways: rules (W), knowing (U),
# favours (B), inspiring (R), custom (G). Options carry lead: <way>, always the way of the option's first letter, so a
# cross X>Y leads X's way toward Y's end; falls carry fall: go|fight|again, handing over hand: chosen|open|stay.
# Crisis and fall moments carry lead_way: the way that is in trouble. No who: line. 25 moments: taking the post 4,
# turn to routine 2, a crisis pair per way 10, a fall per way 5 (calling its own and the next colour), handing over 4.
# Per way, its one-letter option plus its cross (first letter) = 140. Spec chroma-ideas/social-mechanics.md S7.

# Chroma library, modern Earth: WAYS TO LEAD, lead A (S7 ways to lead, v22.3, DRAFT cast_want: lead_take, lead_routine).
# The character is the one in charge at {place} (the Engine fills it with the place's plain name). Every option carries
# lead: <way>, the way of its first letter (W rules, U knowing, B favours, R inspiring, G custom). Chances: per way, its
# one-letter option plus its cross (first letter) = 140. Syntax: header of build.py. Table: tableA.txt.
#   take 1   a new post: how they will hold it        1a WU (s1) / 1b BRG (s2)
#   take 2   the first season in charge               2a BG (s3) / 2b WUR (s4)
#   routine  an inspiring leader, years without a new success   a WR (s1) / b UBG (s2)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- take 1: a new post

== the first morning in charge
tier: everyday
stages: adult mature elder
age: 25-85
calls: WU
rate: 1
tone: mixed
life: work, community
horizon: years
roles: regular, colleague, elder
cast_want: lead_take
earth: you lead at a place you go: you have just been put in charge, and everyone is waiting to see how you will run it
times: People accept a new head for different reasons: rules made properly, knowing best, what the head can give, a spark, or being one of them (Weber 1922; French and Raven 1959)
gap: 3-8
scene: {N} has just been made the one in charge at {place}. On the first morning everyone turns up early, pretending to be busy, watching the door. The last head kept things going for years in a way nobody ever quite explained, and now no one knows what will stay and what will change. Finally one of the regulars asks {N}, half joking, what happens now.
scene W: People work best when they know what is expected and know it is the same for everyone; {N} owes them that from the first day.
scene U: {N} does not yet know how this place really works, and leading it before learning it would be guessing.
scene B: Everyone in the room wants something, and the one who sees what each of them wants will hold the place.
scene R: The room is flat and nervous, and {N} can feel how little it would take to wake it up.
scene G: The place was here long before {N}, and its people will judge {N} by how well {N} fits what it already is.
- W | post the rules plainly where everyone can read them, the same for all | lead: rules | v: conformity, universalism | chance: 75
- U | spend a month learning how the work really runs, then set out a plan | lead: knowing | v: achievement, self-direction | chance: 70
- B | find out who needs what, and quietly see that they get it from you | lead: favours | v: power | mark: made a friend | act: find out who needs what, and quietly see that they get it from them | chance: 75
- R | gather everyone and tell them, with fire, what this place could become | lead: inspiring | v: stimulation, self-direction | chance: 65
- G | keep things as the last head left them, and take the usual seat | lead: custom | v: tradition, conformity | chance: 80
- W>U | set a fair way to test any method, so the best one wins | lead: rules | v: universalism, achievement | chance: 65
- U>B | use what you learn of each person to put them where they can rise | lead: knowing | v: achievement, power | act: use what they learn of each person to put them where they can rise | chance: 70
- B>R | give the bold ones the means and the room to try their own ideas | lead: favours | v: power, stimulation | chance: 65
- R>G | throw a gathering for the whole place, in the old style, to begin your time | lead: inspiring | v: hedonism, tradition | act: throw a gathering for the whole place, in the old style, to begin their time | chance: 75
- G>W | ask the oldest hands how things were always settled, and write it down for all | lead: custom | v: tradition, conformity | chance: 60
works: By the end of the first year the people at {place} speak of how {N} runs things as simply how things are done.
works: One of the regulars who doubted {N} on that first morning now stands up for {N} whenever someone grumbles.
fails: A few of the old hands decide they liked it better before, and they say so, often, where everyone can hear.
fails: The first months are rough, and twice {N} has to undo a decision in front of the whole place.

== the first quarrel on the new head's desk
tier: everyday
stages: adult mature elder
age: 25-85
calls: BRG
rate: 1
tone: mixed
life: work, community
horizon: years
roles: regular, colleague, elder
cast_want: lead_take
earth: you lead at a place you go: in your first week in charge, two people bring you the same quarrel and everyone watches how you settle it
times: The first decisions of a new head set how the place reads them, and a head seen as one of us is followed more readily (Hogg 2001; Haslam, Reicher and Platow 2011)
gap: 3-8
scene: The keys to {place} came to {N} on a quiet evening, with a handshake and not much else. By the end of the first week a quarrel is waiting: two people both want the same thing, the better slot, the bigger share, the first turn, and both say the last head promised it to them. Nobody else is involved, but everyone is listening. Whatever {N} decides will be how {N} is known.
scene W: The quarrel matters less than the way it is settled; whatever {N} does now, {N} must be willing to do for anyone.
scene U: {N} wants the facts first: who actually needs it, who would use it best, what the last head really said.
scene B: Two people want something only {N} can give; whichever way it goes, {N} has the chance to be owed.
scene R: The two of them are tense and proud, and {N} would rather win them both over than pick a loser.
scene G: Places like this have always had their own way of settling such things, and {N} is expected to know it.
- W | settle it by a rule you announce for everyone, starting today | lead: rules | v: universalism, conformity | act: settle it by a rule they announce for everyone, starting today | chance: 70
- U | look into the facts and give it to whoever will make best use of it | lead: knowing | v: achievement | chance: 75
- B | give it to one, and promise the other something better soon | lead: favours | v: power, security | chance: 65
- R | bring them both in and talk them, laughing, into sharing it | lead: inspiring | v: stimulation, benevolence | chance: 70
- G | settle it the way the place has always settled such things | lead: custom | v: tradition | chance: 80
- W>B | set a fair rule that rewards whoever brings in the most | lead: rules | v: achievement, power | chance: 70
- U>R | work out an arrangement that lets each of them do it their own way | lead: knowing | v: self-direction | chance: 65
- B>G | give it to the one with the deepest roots, and let everyone see you honour them | lead: favours | v: tradition, power | mark: made a friend | act: give it to the one with the deepest roots, and let everyone see that honour | chance: 75
- R>W | stand up before everyone and promise, warmly, that every quarrel will get a fair hearing | lead: inspiring | v: universalism, stimulation | chance: 70
- G>U | ask the oldest member how it used to be decided, and why, before you choose | lead: custom | v: tradition, self-direction | act: ask the oldest member how it used to be decided, and why, before choosing | chance: 60
works: The two of them shake hands before the month is out, and the next quarrel comes to {N} sooner, which is a sign of trust.
works: Word goes round {place} that the new head is someone people can bring a problem to.
fails: The one who lost out tells everyone it was decided before {N} ever heard them, and some believe it.
fails: Both of them walk away unhappy, and {N} spends the season mending what one afternoon broke.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- take 2: the first season in charge

== a lean first season in charge
tier: everyday
stages: adult mature elder
age: 25-85
calls: BG
rate: 1
tone: mixed
life: work, money
horizon: years
roles: rival, regular, colleague
cast_want: lead_take
earth: you lead at a place you go: your first season in charge turns out lean, and people start to drift
times: Followers stay with a head while the head can deliver, and a lean season tests whatever the head is followed for (Scott 1972; French and Raven 1959)
gap: 3-8
scene: A season into running {place}, {N} is hit by a lean stretch: less coming in, too much to do, tempers short. A few people have started drifting away, and {rival} says, loudly, that the old head would never have let it come to this. The rest are still here, but they are waiting to see whether {N} can carry them through. What {N} does this season will decide what kind of head {N} becomes.
scene W: When there is less to go round, the worst thing is to share it badly; {N} wants a cut everyone can see is fair.
scene U: Something is going wrong, and somewhere in the figures is the reason; {N} wants to find it before guessing.
scene B: {N} knows who matters most to keeping the place going, and keeping them on side is the whole game this season.
scene R: Hard times are when people need something to believe in, and {N} could give them one.
scene G: The place has had lean seasons before and come through them; the way it did so is still there to follow.
- W | share the lean season out by a fair rule, the same cut for everyone | lead: rules | v: universalism, conformity | chance: 70
- U | go through the figures and fix the one thing that is going wrong | lead: knowing | v: achievement | chance: 70
- B | keep the key people on side with whatever favours you can still give | lead: favours | v: power, security | act: keep the key people on side with whatever favours they can still give | chance: 75
- R | rally everyone behind a bold new venture to carry the place through | lead: inspiring | v: stimulation | chance: 60
- G | get through it the way the place got through hard seasons before | lead: custom | v: tradition, security | chance: 75
- W>R | set a fair rule that frees every member to try one idea of their own | lead: rules | v: self-direction, universalism | chance: 70
- U>G | plan for the long haul, and show everyone why patience will pay | lead: knowing | v: security, tradition | chance: 70
- B>W | call in favours from outside to keep everyone at the place paid and fed | lead: favours | v: benevolence, power | chance: 65
- R>U | fire everyone up to learn a new skill together this season | lead: inspiring | v: stimulation, achievement | chance: 80
- G>B | lean on old loyalties to win back the people drifting away | lead: custom | v: tradition, power | mark: made an enemy | chance: 65
works: The place comes through the season smaller but closer, and the people who stayed now count {N} as their own.
works: When the good times return, nobody at {place} remembers {rival}'s complaints, only that {N} held on.
fails: The lean stretch runs longer than {N} hoped, and a few good people leave for good.
fails: {rival} keeps a tally of every misstep, and by the end of the season others have started to keep it too.

== the colleague who wanted the post
tier: everyday
stages: adult mature elder
age: 25-85
calls: WUR
rate: 1
tone: mixed
life: work, community
horizon: years
roles: colleague, regular, elder
cast_want: lead_take
earth: you lead at a place you go: in your first season in charge, the one who expected the post is quietly running things around you
times: A new head's standing in the first months is fragile, and a rival from inside the group can split it (French and Raven 1959; Haslam, Reicher and Platow 2011)
gap: 3-8
scene: {colleague} expected to be put in charge at {place}, and everyone knows it. A season into {Ns} time, {N} keeps finding small things done differently from what {N} asked, and hears of meetings that {N} was not invited to. Nobody has openly taken a side yet. But the place cannot have two heads for long, and both of them know it.
scene W: If the post is to mean anything, it has to be held openly and answerably, not by whoever has the most quiet meetings.
scene U: {N} thinks the plan is right, and the results so far should show it; the question is whether people will look at them.
scene B: {colleague} wants standing; give {colleague} some, on {Ns} terms, and the trouble may become an asset.
scene R: {N} took this post because people wanted to follow {N}; they need reminding why.
scene G: The place has always closed ranks behind whoever holds the post; {N} needs it to remember that.
- W | put the question to a fair vote of everyone, and abide by it | lead: rules | v: universalism, conformity | mark: kept your word | chance: 65
- U | show the results so far, plainly, and let them speak for your plan | lead: knowing | v: achievement | act: show the results so far, plainly, and let them speak for the plan | chance: 70
- B | offer {colleague} a role of their own that keeps them in your debt | lead: favours | v: power | act: offer {colleague} a role of their own that keeps {colleague} in their debt | chance: 70
- R | win the room back with a speech nobody at the place will forget | lead: inspiring | v: stimulation, self-direction | chance: 65
- G | remind everyone, gently, that the place has always stood by its head | lead: custom | v: tradition, conformity | chance: 75
- W>G | write the place's old customs into its rules, so nobody can push them aside | lead: rules | v: tradition, conformity | chance: 75
- U>W | lay out a clear plan in which every person knows their part and their due | lead: knowing | v: universalism, achievement | chance: 70
- B>U | pay for {colleague} to train further, and win a sharp mind to your side | lead: favours | v: power, achievement | act: pay for {colleague} to train further, and win a sharp mind to their side | chance: 70
- R>B | talk the boldest members into backing you, and leave {colleague} on the outside | lead: inspiring | v: power, stimulation | mark: made an enemy | act: talk the boldest members into backing them, and leave {colleague} on the outside | chance: 75
- G>R | bring back the place's old celebration, and invite everyone, {colleague} included | lead: custom | v: tradition, hedonism | mark: made a friend | chance: 65
works: By the end of the season {colleague} is working with {N}, not around {N}, and the place feels the difference.
works: The people at {place} stop counting heads in private, and the side meetings quietly end.
fails: {colleague} stays, but the place splits into two camps that nod politely at each other in the corridor.
fails: Some of the regulars decide {N} cannot hold the post, and they start bringing their problems to {colleague} instead.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- the turn to routine

== the spark that brought them in
tier: everyday
stages: adult mature elder
age: 25-85
calls: WR
rate: 1
tone: mixed
life: work, community
horizon: years
roles: friend, regular, colleague
cast_want: lead_routine
earth: you lead at a place you go: years ago you filled it with life, but nothing new has landed in a long while and the crowd is thinning
times: A leader followed for a gift must keep proving it, and the gift fades unless it is turned into a lasting order (Weber 1922)
gap: 3-8
scene: Years ago {N} took over {place} and filled it with life: new faces, big nights, a sense that anything could happen there. Lately nothing new has landed, the crowd is thinner, and the loyal ones still come, but more out of habit than hope. Over a late cup {friend} says it plainly: the place needs either a new spark or a way of running that does not depend on {Ns} fire. {N} knows {friend} is right, and hates it a little.
scene W: Something good was built here, and it should not vanish the day {N} gets tired; it needs a shape that lasts.
scene U: Some of the old successes worked and some were luck; {N} wants to know which, before trying anything more.
scene B: The loyal ones are the place's real capital now, and {N} needs to keep them while a new win is found.
scene R: {N} came alive building this; the thought of turning it into a timetable feels like a small death.
scene G: The big nights have become part of what the place is; the people who love it would keep them as they are.
- W | turn what works into a fixed programme, written down, that runs without you | lead: rules | v: security, conformity | act: turn what works into a fixed programme, written down, that runs without them | chance: 70
- U | study which old successes truly worked, and why, and do only those | lead: knowing | v: achievement | chance: 70
- B | keep the loyal ones close with small favours while you look for the next win | lead: favours | v: power, security | act: keep the loyal ones close with small favours while looking for the next win | chance: 70
- R | gamble on one big new venture to light the place up again | lead: inspiring | v: stimulation | chance: 55
- G | let the old nights become the place's traditions, the same each year | lead: custom | v: tradition | chance: 75
- W>U | set up a fixed programme, with a yearly review of what worked and what did not | lead: rules | v: security, achievement | chance: 70
- U>B | plan the next step carefully, and take the best of the place on to something bigger | lead: knowing | v: achievement, power | chance: 70
- B>R | back a younger member's wild idea with your name and your contacts | lead: favours | v: power, stimulation | act: back a younger member's wild idea with their name and their contacts | chance: 70
- R>G | throw one last great night for everyone who ever came, and hand the place to them | lead: inspiring | v: hedonism, benevolence | chance: 85
- G>W | set the old nights down as a calendar everyone can count on and share fairly | lead: custom | v: tradition, conformity | chance: 65
works: The place finds a second life, quieter but steadier, and people still say {Ns} name when they talk about how it began.
works: {friend} tells {N}, a year later, that it was the hardest and best thing {N} ever did for {place}.
fails: Another quiet year goes by, and a few more of the old faces stop coming without saying goodbye.
fails: The change lands badly, and some of the loyal ones say {N} has lost what made the place special.

== the old speech to newer faces
tier: everyday
stages: adult mature elder
age: 25-85
calls: UBG
rate: 1
tone: mixed
life: work, community
horizon: years
roles: mentor, regular, colleague
cast_want: lead_routine
earth: you lead at a place you go: you give the old speech that once moved everyone, and the newer faces are checking the time
times: A gift that brought people in must keep being proved, or be turned into a settled way of doing things that outlasts the leader (Weber 1922)
gap: 3-8
scene: At the yearly gathering of {place}, {N} gives the speech that once brought people to their feet. The first followers still smile at the familiar lines, but the newer faces are checking the time, and it has been a few years since anything new came of it. Afterwards {mentor} says, kindly, that a fire cannot be the whole plan forever. {N} goes home and cannot stop thinking about it.
scene W: What {N} started deserves a form that holds, so it does not rest on one person's mood on one night.
scene U: The newer faces want something {N} has not yet understood; finding out what is the first job.
scene B: The newer faces owe the place nothing yet; give them a stake, and they will.
scene R: {N} used to feel the room lift; tonight it did not, and {N} misses it more than {N} will say.
scene G: The first followers are the heart of the place, and what holds them is worth more than winning over strangers.
- W | draw up a fixed timetable and rota, so the place runs the same each week | lead: rules | v: security, conformity | chance: 75
- U | ask the newer faces what draws them, and rebuild the plan from what you learn | lead: knowing | v: self-direction, achievement | act: ask the newer faces what draws them, and rebuild the plan from what they learn | chance: 65
- B | give the newcomers small roles and perks, so they feel the place is theirs | lead: favours | v: power | chance: 70
- R | rewrite the old speech from scratch and give it with all you have | lead: inspiring | v: stimulation, self-direction | act: rewrite the old speech from scratch and give it with everything they have | chance: 60
- G | keep the gathering as it always was; the ones who belong will stay | lead: custom | v: tradition | chance: 70
- W>B | set fair rules for how anyone can rise to run a part of the place | lead: rules | v: achievement, conformity | chance: 65
- U>R | plan a season of open nights where anyone can try something new | lead: knowing | v: stimulation, self-direction | chance: 75
- B>G | call in old favours to give the place a lasting home of its own | lead: favours | v: power, security | chance: 70
- R>W | rally the old crowd to run the place by turns, with fair shares for all | lead: inspiring | v: universalism, stimulation | mark: made a friend | chance: 80
- G>U | sit with the first followers and work out together what made the early years work | lead: custom | v: tradition, achievement | chance: 70
works: The next gathering is fuller than it has been in years, and some of the newer faces stay late to help clear up.
works: {mentor} catches {Ns} eye across the room and nods, and {N} knows the place will outlast its founder.
fails: The change pleases nobody fully: the first followers feel pushed aside, and the newer faces still drift.
fails: By the next gathering a few more chairs are empty, and {N} gives the old speech to a smaller room.

# Chroma library, modern Earth: WAYS TO LEAD, crisis part B (S7 ways to lead, v22.3, DRAFT cast_want: lead_crisis).
# The character leads {place}; the Engine fills {place} with the place's plain name. Two crisis pairs (tableB.txt):
#   rules a WB (s3) / rules b URG (s4): a favour that touches the leader; a fairness scandal among the led
#   knowing a UR (s1) / knowing b WBG (s2): a visible mistake of the leader's own judgement
# Every option carries lead: <the way of its first letter>. Chances: per way, its one-letter option plus its cross
# (first letter) = 140. Syntax: header of build.py.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- crisis pair: rules

== the favour signed by the one who keeps the rules
tier: everyday
stages: adult mature elder
age: 25-85
calls: WB
rate: 1
tone: mixed
life: work, community
horizon: years
roles: friend, colleague, regular
cast_want: lead_crisis
lead_way: rules
earth: you lead at a place you go: you have always held it by one set of rules for everyone, and now a favour with your name on it has come out
times: Authority held by rules made in due form lasts while the rules are seen to bind the one who keeps them; a favour to a friend breaks that faster than any outside attack (Weber 1922)
gap: 3-8
scene: {N} has led {place} for years by one list and one set of rules, the same for everyone. This week it comes out that {friend} was moved up the list ahead of others, and the signature on the paper is {Ns}. It was a small kindness in a hard week, signed without much thought. Now people are asking whether the rules ever applied to the friends of the one who keeps them.
scene W: {N} built {Ns} standing on the rules binding everyone, {N} included; if that is not true, nothing else {N} says will hold.
scene U: {N} can see exactly where the system let one signature move the list, and that flaw matters more than the one case.
scene B: Everyone at {place} has had something from {N} at some point; the question is who remembers it now.
scene R: {N} helped a friend in trouble, and part of {N} refuses to be ashamed of that, whatever the rules say.
scene G: In a place like this, people judge the person they have known for years, not one piece of paper.
- W | put the matter before everyone at {place} and accept whatever the rules decide | lead: rules | v: universalism, conformity | mark: owned up | chance: 75
- U | redesign the list so no single signature, yours included, can ever move it again | lead: knowing | v: achievement, self-direction | act: redesign the list so no single signature, their own included, can ever move it again | chance: 70
- B | call in what people owe you, quietly, so the complaint fades before the meeting | lead: favours | v: power | mark: hid a wrong | act: call in what people owe them, quietly, so the complaint fades before the meeting | chance: 80
- R | tell everyone plainly that you helped a friend in trouble, and ask them to judge you | lead: inspiring | v: benevolence, self-direction | act: tell everyone plainly that they helped a friend in trouble, and ask them to judge | chance: 60
- G | go round the old hands one by one, as one of them, and talk it through | lead: custom | v: tradition, conformity | chance: 75
- W>R | write a fair rule for hardship cases, so a kindness like this can be given openly | lead: rules | v: benevolence, universalism | chance: 65
- U>G | find how the place handled such cases in the old days, and bring that practice back | lead: knowing | v: tradition, self-direction | chance: 70
- B>W | offer each person who was passed over a favour of their choosing, to square the account | lead: favours | v: power, universalism | chance: 60
- R>U | call an open evening where everyone rebuilds the list together, from scratch | lead: inspiring | v: stimulation, self-direction | chance: 80
- G>B | remind the oldest families what you have done for them, and let them close ranks | lead: custom | v: tradition, power | act: remind the oldest families what they have done for them, and let them close ranks | chance: 65
works: The talk dies down within a season, and people at {place} say {N} came out of it the same person they always trusted.
works: {friend} thanks {N} quietly, and one of the loudest critics admits the place is in steadier hands than they thought.
fails: The story keeps going round {place}, and every decision {N} makes for a year is checked twice by someone.
fails: Two of the old regulars stop coming to meetings, and {friend} keeps a careful distance from {N}.

== the duties some were quietly excused from
tier: everyday
stages: adult mature elder
age: 25-85
calls: URG
rate: 1
tone: mixed
life: work, community
horizon: years
roles: colleague, regular, rival
cast_want: lead_crisis
lead_way: rules
earth: you lead at a place you go: you hold it by rules everyone agreed to, and now it turns out a few have been quietly excused from them
times: Rules hold people together while they are seen as fair; a group that finds some members excused from shared duties loses trust in the leader who keeps the rules (Weber 1922; French and Raven 1959)
gap: 3-8
scene: {N} leads {place} by rules everyone agreed to, and has always said they are the same for everyone. Then {colleague} brings proof that for a year a few people have been quietly excused from the dull duties while the rest covered for them. The ones who covered are angry, and some say the rules were only ever for people without friends. Everyone is waiting to see whether {N} holds the line.
scene W: A rule that some keep and others dodge is worse than no rule at all; {N} has to make it bind everyone again.
scene U: {N} wants to know exactly how it happened and who carried what, before anyone is judged.
scene B: The ones who were excused are also the ones with the most pull at {place}; {N} weighs what a fight with them would cost.
scene R: The anger in the room is real, and {N} feels it too; people need to see that someone cares how they were treated.
scene G: Places like this have always had their quiet arrangements; {N} wonders whether this one is a betrayal or just the old way gone too far.
- W | call everyone together, read out the rule, and apply it to every name, friends included | lead: rules | v: conformity, universalism | chance: 70
- U | work out exactly who owes how many duties, and publish a fair plan to settle it | lead: knowing | v: achievement, universalism | chance: 75
- B | let the excused ones keep their peace, in exchange for their backing when you need it | lead: favours | v: power | mark: hid a wrong | act: let the excused ones keep their peace, in exchange for their backing when needed | chance: 80
- R | speak to everyone from the heart about trust, and ask the excused ones to step forward | lead: inspiring | v: benevolence, stimulation | chance: 60
- G | sit down with the old hands and settle it the way {place} always has | lead: custom | v: tradition | chance: 70
- W>G | put the old unwritten habit of sharing the dull duties into the rules, by a vote | lead: rules | v: tradition, conformity | chance: 70
- U>W | design a rota nobody can dodge, and walk everyone through how it works | lead: knowing | v: universalism, achievement | chance: 65
- B>U | offer the excused ones a quiet deal: explain how it worked, and nothing more is said | lead: favours | v: power, self-direction | chance: 60
- R>B | make the scandal your cause, and ride the anger to a bigger say at {place} | lead: inspiring | v: power, stimulation | act: make the scandal their cause, and ride the anger to a bigger say at {place} | chance: 80
- G>R | hold the end-of-season gathering early, so people can laugh together and clear the air | lead: custom | v: tradition, hedonism | chance: 70
works: The dull duties are shared out again, and people at {place} start to say that under {N} the rules mean something.
works: {colleague}, who brought the proof, tells others that {N} handled it better than anyone expected.
fails: The ones who covered say nothing has really changed, and the grumbling at {place} goes on for months.
fails: {rival} uses the affair to gather a small following of their own, and some of {Ns} oldest backers drift towards them.

