== the purse that kept the turns coming runs dry
tier: everyday
stages: adult mature elder
age: 25-85
calls: BG
rate: 1
tone: mixed
life: work, community
horizon: years
roles: regular, colleague, boss
cast_want: lead_crisis
lead_way: favours
earth: you lead at a place you go: you kept it going by doing people turns, and now the means to do them has run out
times: A patron's following lasts only as long as the patron can keep delivering; when the means dry up, the clients drift to whoever still can (Scott 1972)
gap: 3-8
scene: For years {N} has held {place} together with favours: a loan to tide someone over, a word in the right ear, a place found for somebody's cousin. This season the purse {N} used to dip into has been cut to nothing, and the people who once returned {Ns} calls have stopped picking up. The regulars still come with their troubles, and {N} has nothing left to hand them. Two of them have already been seen talking to someone who still has means.
scene W: Favours were always a little crooked, {N} thinks; perhaps this is the moment to put what is left on a fair footing.
scene U: {N} sits down with the figures. If the turns are gone, something else has to do their work, and it had better be thought through.
scene B: {N} built this place on what people owed. Debts are still debts, even when the purse is empty; it is time to see who remembers.
scene R: It hurts that people {N} helped are already looking elsewhere. {N} wants to believe they stayed for more than what {N} could give.
scene G: Before there was any purse, people here simply helped each other. {N} remembers how that went, and wonders if it still could.
- W | share what little is left by a clear list, the same for everyone | lead: rules | v: universalism, conformity | chance: 70
- U | work out exactly what {place} can afford now, and show everyone the plan | lead: knowing | v: achievement, self-direction | chance: 75
- B | call in what people owe you, and use it to find new money | lead: favours | v: power | act: call in what people owe them, and use it to find new money | chance: 60
- R | gather everyone, admit the purse is empty, and ask them to stay anyway | lead: inspiring | v: stimulation, benevolence | mark: owned up | chance: 65
- G | go back to the old way, before the money: neighbours pitching in for each other | lead: custom | v: tradition, benevolence | chance: 75
- W>R | write a fair rule that nobody owes anybody, so people come because they want to | lead: rules | v: universalism, self-direction | chance: 70
- U>G | study how {place} got by in leaner years, and rebuild that way, smarter | lead: knowing | v: tradition, achievement | chance: 65
- B>W | spend your last favours winning {place} a steady, fair budget of its own | lead: favours | v: power, security | act: spend their last favours winning {place} a steady, fair budget of its own | chance: 80
- R>U | fire people up to learn to do for themselves what your favours did for them | lead: inspiring | v: stimulation, achievement | act: fire people up to learn to do for themselves what the favours did for them | chance: 75
- G>B | ask the old hands, as one of them, to pool their means and stand alone | lead: custom | v: tradition, power | chance: 65
works: Within a year {place} finds its feet without the old purse, and people say {N} handled the lean time well.
works: One of the regulars {N} once helped quietly comes back and offers help of their own, unasked.
fails: The drift goes on: by the next season half the regulars have taken their troubles to someone else.
fails: Word goes round {place} that {N} has lost the knack, and {Ns} say carries less weight in every meeting.

