- B | strike a deal with someone who still has means, and keep the turns coming | lead: favours | v: power, achievement | chance: 60
- R | tell them plainly and warmly: you stayed for more than favours, so stay now | lead: inspiring | v: stimulation, benevolence | chance: 65
- G | sit with the old hands and remind them what {place} has always meant to them | lead: custom | v: tradition | chance: 70
- W>G | write the old ways down as rules, so {place} no longer hangs on one person | lead: rules | v: conformity, tradition | chance: 65
- U>W | draw up a plan, with the figures, for sharing what is left fairly | lead: knowing | v: universalism, achievement | chance: 70
- B>U | call in your last favours to get the regulars trained in what you used to fix | lead: favours | v: power, self-direction | act: call in their last favours to get the regulars trained in what they used to fix | chance: 80
- R>B | rally the regulars behind you to win the lost post back | lead: inspiring | v: stimulation, power | act: rally the regulars behind them to win the lost post back | mark: made an enemy | chance: 75
- G>R | bring back the old feast days, so people come for joy, not favours | lead: custom | v: tradition, hedonism | chance: 70
works: The regulars stay, and by the turn of the year {rival} has stopped trying to win them over.
works: Someone at {place} tells {N}, almost shyly, that they never came for the favours in the first place.
fails: {rival} wins over the busiest regulars, and {place} splits into those who stayed with {N} and those who did not.
