== keeping the old ways as the place changes fast
tier: everyday
stages: adult mature elder
age: 25-85
calls: UG
rate: 1
tone: mixed
life: community, place
horizon: years
roles: elder, regular, rival
cast_want: lead_crisis
lead_way: custom
earth: you lead at a place you go: the place has changed fast, and the old ways you keep no longer fit everyone
times: Leaders seen as one of us keep their following, until the group itself changes and the old picture of one of us no longer fits (Hogg 2001)
gap: 3-8
scene: For years {N} has led {place} as one of its own, keeping the ways the older members taught. In the last two years half the faces are new, the work has changed, and the old ways no longer fit how things are done. At the season's meeting a newcomer asks, politely, why things are done this way at all, and no answer satisfies the new people. The older members look to {N}, and so do the new ones.
scene W: Old members and new ones deserve the same say, and {N} wonders whether custom alone can still be fair to both.
scene U: {N} can see which old ways still do their job and which only feel familiar; sorting them is the real task.
scene B: The new people are half the place now, and a leader who loses them loses the place. {N} counts who needs what.
scene R: The meeting had no life in it. The old gatherings used to fill the room, and {N} misses that more than any rule.
scene G: These ways held the place together longer than anyone remembers. {N} feels the ground move and does not want to be the one who lets go.
- W | write the old ways down as rules, and let the whole place vote on each one | lead: rules | v: universalism, conformity | chance: 65
- U | study which old ways still work, and keep only those, with the reasons given | lead: knowing | v: self-direction, achievement | chance: 70
- B | give the newcomers' leading voices a seat and a say, in return for their backing | lead: favours | v: power | mark: made a friend | chance: 75
- R | throw a big open gathering where old members teach new ones the place's ways | lead: inspiring | v: stimulation, benevolence | chance: 60
- G | hold to the old ways, patiently, and trust the newcomers to grow into them | lead: custom | v: tradition | chance: 55
- W>R | set fair rules for a yearly celebration that keeps the place's old spirit alive | lead: rules | v: conformity, stimulation | chance: 75
- U>G | find out what each old way was for, and keep that purpose in a new form | lead: knowing | v: tradition, self-direction | chance: 70
- B>W | bargain for a written charter: posts for the newcomers, the core ways kept for all | lead: favours | v: power, security | chance: 65
- R>U | stand up and tell how each custom began, then invite everyone to question it | lead: inspiring | v: stimulation, self-direction | chance: 80
- G>B | ask the oldest families to take a newcomer each under their wing, the old way | lead: custom | v: tradition, power | chance: 85
works: The place settles into a new shape that old and new members can both call their own, and {N} is still the one they look to.
works: At the next gathering a newcomer does one of the old things without being asked, and one of the older members notices.
fails: Half the older members feel betrayed and half the new ones feel shut out, and the meetings grow thin.
fails: Someone else starts calling meetings of their own at {place}, and people start going to those instead.

